  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe main focus for the long-term forecast period continues to be on \nbuilding heat and humidity, especially for Monday through at least \nWednesday before relatively cooler conditions return by the end of \nthe week. For areas that avoid any precipitation during this period, \nconfidence is higher for very warm and muggy conditions. The latest \nECMWF EFI/Shift of Tails values for high temperatures Monday through \nWednesday are in excess of 0.9 (even some values over 0.95 in \nisolated spots), along with Shift of Tails values around 1. This \nsuggests high confidence in anomalously warm high temperatures, \nalthough not extreme values based on the lower Shift of Tails. In \nterms of heat headlines, we will need to watch this closely, given \nthe LREF ensemble exceedance probabilities of heat indices of 100 \ndegree or warmer is around 30 to 50% for areas along and south of \nHighway 92 for Wednesday and Thursday. The probabilities of heat \nindices of 95 degree or warmer goes up drastically, to around 60 to \n90% over most of our forecast area. Additionally, overnight lows \nmight not dip below 75 degrees Monday night through Wednesday night, \nso this would continue stress from the heat - even at night. There \nis potential for some record warm low temperatures during this \nperiod. See the Climate section below for the current records. We \nwill hold off on headlines for now, but Heat Advisories may be \nneeded next week.\n\nWith all of this said, it bears repeating that any showers or storms \nthat develop will help to limit temperatures, and therefore, heat \nindices. Ultimately, this appears most likely for areas along and \nnorth of Highway 30, where both NBM and LREF ensemble probabilities \nof measurable rainfall are 40 to 60%. In terms of strong to severe \nstorms, there are some signals in the various machine-learning \nsevere probs for at least a limited potential on Wednesday and \nThursday as the mid-level ridge becomes more zonal for a time and a \nweak cold front sweeps through the area during that time with \nappreciable instability. Something for us to keep an eye on, but \nwith it being further out, confidence isn't particularly high on \nsevere weather at this time.",
